---
import FaqBlock from '../components/FaqBlock.astro';
import SourcePanel from '../components/SourcePanel.astro';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { ASSETS } from '../data/assets';
import { SOURCES } from '../data/sources';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';

const title = 'Grave Seasons Release Date - Fall 2026 Window & Platforms';
const description =
  'Grave Seasons is not out. Blumhouse lists Fall 2026. Steam lists March 9, 2027 and is still coming soon.';
const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Release Date', url: '/release-date/' },
];
const sources = [SOURCES.steamDevlog, SOURCES.steam, SOURCES.blumhouse, SOURCES.playstation, SOURCES.xbox];

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'When does Grave Seasons come out?',
    answer:
      'Not released. Blumhouse still lists Fall 2026. Steam App 3255110, checked October 18, 2026, lists March 9, 2027 and coming soon. The June 5 studio post moved the old August 14 date to Fall 2026. The Steam day is a store field, not a replacement for that post until the publisher says so.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why was Grave Seasons delayed from August 14?',
    answer:
      'The studio\'s own 5 June 2026 Steam post moved the full game to Fall 2026 across all announced platforms, and also cancelled the demo that had been planned for June 15. Perfect Garbage framed it as needing more time rather than pointing at any single problem.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why do Steam and Blumhouse show different release timing?',
    answer:
      'They are no longer the same sentence. Blumhouse still says Fall 2026. Steam now lists March 9, 2027. Keep both, with the date each one was checked. Do not delete the Fall 2026 post just because the store field changed.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What platforms is Grave Seasons releasing on?',
    answer:
      'Four, named in the official announcement: PC, Xbox Series X|S, PlayStation 5, and Nintendo Switch. Live store listings currently exist for Steam (app id 3255110), PlayStation Store, and Microsoft Store; a Switch eShop page is not tracked yet even though the platform is confirmed.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is Grave Seasons coming to Game Pass?',
    answer:
      'Yes. Perfect Garbage stated that Grave Seasons "will also be released on Day 1 for Xbox Game Pass players." The delay post kept all announced platforms, so that commitment stands. Which Game Pass tiers it lands on has not been specified.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is there a demo, beta, or preorder?',
    answer:
      'Not currently. A demo had been planned for June 15, 2026 but was cancelled in the same post that delayed the full game; the studio said it would share more when it had news. No preorder or price has been published on any store page, so wishlisting or following is the only action available.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Could the Fall 2026 window slip?',
    answer:
      'Yes. This is already the second date, and a season-level window with no locked day is exactly the kind of target that moves. This page records what the official pages say on the date shown, and changes when they change.',
  },
];
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} image={ASSETS.steamHeader.src}>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
    <p class="section-kicker">Release tracker</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-black leading-tight text-bone md:text-5xl">
      Grave Seasons Release Date
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-fog">
      <strong class="text-bone">Grave Seasons is not released.</strong> Perfect Garbage moved the
      original August 14, 2026 date to <strong class="text-bone">{SITE.releaseWindow}</strong> on
      5 June 2026. Blumhouse still lists that window. Steam App 3255110, checked October 18, 2026,
      lists <strong class="text-bone">{SITE.steamReleaseText}</strong> and is still coming soon.
      Both dates stay on this page.
    </p>

    <section class="mt-8 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-3">
      <div class="surface p-5">
        <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase text-wheat">Official window</p>
        <h2 class="mt-2 text-xl font-black text-bone">{SITE.releaseWindow}</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-fog">
          Moved here from August 14, 2026 by Perfect Garbage's 5 June 2026 Steam post.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="surface p-5">
        <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase text-wheat">Steam field</p>
        <h2 class="mt-2 text-xl font-black text-bone">{SITE.steamReleaseText}</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-fog">
          App {SITE.appId} lists the year only. A firm date usually appears here first.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="surface p-5">
        <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase text-wheat">Team</p>
        <h2 class="mt-2 text-xl font-black text-bone">{SITE.developer}</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-fog">
          Developed by {SITE.developer}, published by {SITE.publisher}.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="prose-page mt-10">
      <h2>What is actually confirmed about the launch</h2>
      <p>
        Grave Seasons is a narrative farming sim with a supernatural serial killer hidden among the
        residents of Ashenridge. The Steam page commits to a specific structure: you have
        <strong>one year</strong> to protect potential victims and work out who is behind the murders,
        across a town of <strong>30+ residents</strong>, <strong>70+ interiors</strong>, and
        <strong>seven major locations</strong>. Twelve of those residents are romanceable, and the
        store copy states outright that some of them may be the killer.
      </p>
      <p>
        The replay hook is that each playthrough picks the killer from a candidate pool, with
        killer-specific motives, kill styles, and targets. That is the pitch you will be buying into
        at launch, and it is all sourced from official store copy rather than previews.
      </p>

      <h2>The date history, in the studio's own words</h2>
      <p>
        On <strong>26 March 2026</strong> Perfect Garbage announced that "Grave Seasons will launch on
        August 14, 2026 for PC, Xbox Series X|S, PlayStation 5, and Nintendo Switch," and added that it
        "will also be released on Day 1 for Xbox Game Pass players."
      </p>
      <p>
        On <strong>5 June 2026</strong> the studio posted that it was "moving the release date of the
        full game to Fall of this year," <strong>across all announced platforms</strong>, and that the
        demo planned for <strong>June 15</strong> was cancelled. That is the current state: same four
        platforms, same Game Pass commitment, later window, no demo.
      </p>

      <h2>What is not announced yet</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>One agreed release date.</strong> Fall 2026 is the publisher window. March 9, 2027 is the current Steam field. The game is still coming soon.</li>
        <li><strong>Price.</strong> No figure on Steam, PlayStation, or Microsoft Store.</li>
        <li><strong>Editions or preorder bonuses.</strong> Nothing published.</li>
        <li><strong>A demo or beta.</strong> The June 15 demo was cancelled and not rescheduled.</li>
        <li><strong>Game Pass tiers.</strong> Day 1 Game Pass is confirmed; which tiers is not.</li>
        <li><strong>A Switch eShop listing.</strong> The platform is confirmed; the store page is not live yet.</li>
        <li><strong>PC system requirements.</strong> Not published on the Steam page.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Leaving these blank is deliberate. A farming-sim launch generates enormous search volume for
        "release date", "price", and "Game Pass", and the temptation is to fill the gaps with
        plausible guesses. We would rather say "unannounced" and be right.
      </p>

      <h2>Where a date will appear first</h2>
      <p>
        In practice, publishers lock the Steam release field before or alongside a marketing beat,
        because wishlist notification emails depend on it. Watching the
        <a href={SITE.steamUrl} target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">Steam page</a> for
        app {SITE.appId} is the highest-signal move. Blumhouse's own
        <a href={SITE.officialUrl} target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">game page</a>
        typically carries the announcement copy, and the
        <a href="/platforms/">platforms page</a> here tracks all three store listings side by side.
      </p>

      <h2>How to prepare before launch</h2>
      <p>
        The most useful pre-launch reading is the material that will not change when a date is
        announced: the twelve confirmed romance options and the risk that one of them is the killer
        (<a href="/romance-guide/">romance guide</a>), the one-year structure the investigation runs
        on (<a href="/walkthrough/">walkthrough framework</a>), the resident roster
        (<a href="/residents/">residents</a>), and the planning tips that avoid claiming untested
        mechanics (<a href="/tips/">tips</a>).
      </p>

      <h2>How this page is maintained</h2>
      <p>
        Every claim above traces to one of the official sources listed at the bottom of this
        page, last checked {SITE.lastVerified}. When the window narrows to a date, this page leads
        with the date, keeps the old window visible so you can see what changed, and updates the
        source date. Where two official sources sit at different precision — as the studio's Fall 2026
        post and Steam's bare "2026" field do right now — both are shown rather than picking the one
        that reads better.
      </p>
    </section>

    <FaqBlock items={faqs} />

    <SourcePanel sources={sources} />
  </article>
</Layout>
